    crashed handler.
  - [3mCorrupt or absent stored JSON.[0m [38;5;153mgetSession()[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m for "nothing stored" and, on a [38;5;153mJSON.parse[39m failure, clears
    the bad key and also returns [38;5;153mnull[39m. Callers get one answer to "am I logged in" and never see a parse exception.
  - [3mAuth failure.[0m The shape exists ([38;5;153m{ ok: false, error }[39m) though the stub never produces it. Step 4 above is the path,
    so real auth doesn't arrive to find no handling.

  Reporting stays on [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m/[38;5;153mconsole.error[39m, matching what [38;5;153mapp.js[39m does today — you didn't ask for UI changes, and
  inventing an error banner is scope I shouldn't take.

  [1mTesting[0m — Node's built-in runner, [38;5;153mnode --test[39m, plus [38;5;153m"scripts": { "test": "node --test" }[39m. Zero dependencies, nothing
  to install, which keeps the repo's dependency-free character.

  [38;5;153msession.js[39m reads [38;5;153mglobalThis.sessionStorage[39m [3minside[0m each function rather than capturing it at import. That's one small
  deliberate choice: tests can install a fake storage object on [38;5;153mglobalThis[39m with no dependency-injection plumbing in the
  production code, and it's the same seam that later lets you swap to [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m.

  Planned cases: round-trip save → [38;5;153mgetUserId[39m; [38;5;153mgetUserId[39m with nothing stored → [38;5;153mnull[39m; corrupt JSON → [38;5;153mnull[39m [1mand[0m key cleared;
  [38;5;153mclearSession[39m removes the key; write failure → returns [38;5;153mfalse[39m, doesn't throw; stub [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns the same id twice for
  one username and different ids across usernames; [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m rejects each missing field.

  [1mHonest limit:[0m the submit handler itself gets no automated coverage — it needs a DOM, and you declined end-to-end
  tests. Everything it calls is covered; the wiring between them is verified by hand.

